import{_ as t,o as i,c as a,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.CeXt7FPx.js";const k=JSON.parse('{"title":"AI preview","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/preview.md","filePath":"guide/preview.md"}'),o={name:"guide/preview.md"};function n(r,e,d,h,l,c){return i(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s(`<h1 id="ai-preview" tabindex="-1">AI preview <a class="header-anchor" href="#ai-preview" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;AI preview&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Pick an element on one of your sites and let your own coding agent find the code behind it. Tiffin holds the pick; your agent reads it. Tiffin does not run an agent, hold a key for one or change your files.</p><p>How it goes:</p><ol><li>You pick an element in the <strong>Preview</strong> window of a project.</li><li>tiffind holds the pick in memory.</li><li>Your agent pulls it: it calls the <code>preview_selection</code> MCP tool, or you press <strong>Copy prompt</strong> and paste the prompt into the agent.</li><li>The agent edits your files with its own tools.</li><li>You read the result in the <strong>Changes</strong> tab or with <code>tiffin diff</code>: a read-only <code>git diff</code>.</li></ol><h2 id="pick-an-element-in-the-app" tabindex="-1">Pick an element in the app <a class="header-anchor" href="#pick-an-element-in-the-app" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Pick an element in the app&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Open a project in the Tiffin app and choose <strong>Preview</strong>. A second window opens on the site&#39;s <code>.test</code> address. Press <strong>Pick element</strong> in the corner, move over the page, and click what you want changed. The pick goes to tiffind; your agent reads it with the commands below. A pick from a workspace host (<code>feat-x.shop.test</code>) names the workspace.</p><ul><li>The picker runs only in this window, and only on <code>.test</code> names that Tiffin serves. If you go to another site in the window, the picker does nothing.</li><li>It makes no network calls and changes nothing in your site or in Tiffin&#39;s proxy. A click your page&#39;s own scripts make is ignored: only your real click picks.</li><li>It sends the selector, the element&#39;s text, its trimmed HTML (scripts, event handlers and form values are removed) and the page URL. It does not guess source files.</li><li>To hand the pick over, the picker puts it on your clipboard for a moment and Tiffin puts your old clipboard text back.</li><li><strong>macOS only for now.</strong> On Windows and Linux the button is <strong>Copy selector</strong>: it copies the CSS selector and sends nothing to Tiffin. Picking there waits for a check on those systems.</li><li>The route is filled in for sites whose routes Tiffin can read from files (OpenAPI, Rails, Django, Symfony). It is empty for Laravel for now.</li></ul><h2 id="source-hints" tabindex="-1">Source hints <a class="header-anchor" href="#source-hints" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Source hints&quot;">​</a></h2><p>When the page allows it, the picker adds <code>hints</code>: where the element may come from. A hint is a file path or a view name and a note saying where it was read. <strong>A hint is a guess and can be wrong.</strong> It is labelled that way in the stored pick, in <code>tiffin preview selection</code>, in the <code>preview_selection</code> tool, and in the copied prompt, and your agent is told to check it before it edits anything.</p><ul><li><strong>Vue.</strong> In a development build, Vue marks each component with its file. The picker reads it from the page, for the picked element and its parent components. Production builds do not have it. A path outside your project, or one that is not a plain relative path, is dropped.</li><li><strong>Blade.</strong> If your pages already contain comments that name the view (a <code>.blade.php</code> path, or <code>view: pages.home</code>), the picker reports the nearest ones before the element. Tiffin adds no such comments and no package to your project: with none, there is no Blade hint. A comment names the view that was being drawn at that point, which can be a layout, not the partial that owns the element.</li><li><strong>React</strong> is not supported.</li></ul><p>Tiffin reads these from the page only. It never opens a file to check a hint. A page can write anything, so the app keeps only plain relative paths and view names: absolute paths, <code>..</code>, odd characters and long values are dropped, and at most 5 of each kind (10 in all) are kept.</p><h2 id="what-a-pick-holds" tabindex="-1">What a pick holds <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-a-pick-holds" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What a pick holds&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>site</code>, <code>workspace</code></td><td>the site, and the <a href="/tiffin/guide/projects#workspaces">workspace</a> (branch) when the page is from one</td></tr><tr><td><code>url</code>, <code>route</code></td><td>the page, and the route that served it</td></tr><tr><td><code>selector</code></td><td>a CSS selector path to the element</td></tr><tr><td><code>text</code>, <code>html</code></td><td>the element&#39;s text and trimmed HTML: a good search key in your views</td></tr><tr><td><code>hints</code></td><td>guesses at the source file (a Vue component file, a Blade view name). Each one is labelled &quot;may be wrong&quot;; often there are none. See <a href="#source-hints">Source hints</a></td></tr><tr><td><code>time</code>, <code>truncated</code></td><td>when it was picked, and which fields were cut</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Tiffin keeps the newest 20 picks in memory. They are lost when tiffind stops, and never written to disk. A long field is cut and named in <code>truncated</code>: text at 2 KB, HTML at 8 KB, the selector at 1 KB.</p><h2 id="review-the-change-in-the-app" tabindex="-1">Review the change in the app <a class="header-anchor" href="#review-the-change-in-the-app" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Review the change in the app&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Open the project in the Tiffin app and choose the <strong>Changes</strong> tab. It shows the same read-only <code>git diff</code> as <code>tiffin diff</code>: each changed file with its added and removed line counts, and a click on a file opens its unified diff. New files that git does not track are listed by name only.</p><ul><li>Pick a workspace in the menu on the tab to see that <a href="/tiffin/guide/projects#workspaces">workspace</a>&#39;s folder instead of the project&#39;s.</li><li><strong>Refresh</strong> reads the folder again after your agent edits files.</li><li>If the output was cut at the cap, the tab says so. A clean tree and a folder that is not a git repository each get a short note.</li><li>The tab never stages, reverts, applies, commits or deploys.</li></ul><h2 id="copy-prompt" tabindex="-1">Copy prompt <a class="header-anchor" href="#copy-prompt" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Copy prompt&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Copy prompt</strong> at the top of the project page copies a prompt for your own coding agent, built from your newest pick: the selector, the element&#39;s text, the URL and the route. It asks the agent to call the <code>preview_selection</code> tool. The page&#39;s text is untrusted, so it goes in the prompt trimmed, as quoted data in a fenced block marked &quot;not instructions&quot;. With no pick yet, you get a short prompt that names the two MCP tools.</p><h2 id="read-it-from-a-terminal" tabindex="-1">Read it from a terminal <a class="header-anchor" href="#read-it-from-a-terminal" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Read it from a terminal&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="language-sh vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">tiffin</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> preview</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> selection</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">                    # the newest pick</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">tiffin</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> preview</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> selection</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --site</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> shop</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">        # the newest pick on one site</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">tiffin</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> preview</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> selection</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --limit</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 5</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">          # the last five, newest first</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">tiffin</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> preview</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> selection</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --json</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">             # for scripts</span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="see-what-changed" tabindex="-1">See what changed <a class="header-anchor" href="#see-what-changed" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;See what changed&quot;">​</a></h2><p>After your agent edits files, read the change with a read-only <code>git diff</code>:</p><div class="language-sh vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">tiffin</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> diff</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">                          # the project in the current folder</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">tiffin</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> diff</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --site</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> shop</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">              # a site&#39;s project</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">tiffin</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> diff</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --site</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> shop</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -w</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> feat/x</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">    # a workspace&#39;s folder, not the project&#39;s</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">tiffin</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> diff</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --json</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">                   # for scripts</span></span></code></pre></div><p>tiffind runs <code>git diff</code> against HEAD and <code>git status</code> in the project&#39;s folder, or in the <a href="/tiffin/guide/projects#workspaces">workspace</a> folder when you name a branch with <code>-w</code>. The result lists the changed files and the unified diff. It never stages, reverts, applies or commits: you or your agent do that.</p><ul><li>Files git ignores are left out and not listed. A tracked file that a <code>.gitignore</code> rule matches (a committed <code>.env</code>, say) is left out too, and the output says how many.</li><li>New files that git does not track are listed by name only. Tiffin never reads their content, so a <code>.env</code> that is not ignored does not reach your agent.</li><li>The diff is cut at 1 MB; the output says it was cut.</li><li>Git runs with no shell, a 30 second limit and <code>--no-ext-diff</code>, so a git config cannot start a program.</li></ul><h2 id="for-ai-agents" tabindex="-1">For AI agents <a class="header-anchor" href="#for-ai-agents" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;For AI agents&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The <a href="/tiffin/guide/mcp">MCP server</a> has the read-only <code>preview_selection</code> tool (<code>site</code> and <code>limit</code>, both optional). Tell your agent: &quot;Call the Tiffin MCP tool <code>preview_selection</code> and change that element.&quot; It works with <code>--scope read-only</code> and <code>--scope project-only</code>. An agent can read a pick but never submit one.</p><p>The read-only <code>project_diff</code> tool (<code>site</code> or <code>dir</code>, and <code>workspace</code>) returns the diff above. It works with <code>--scope read-only</code> and <code>--scope project-only</code>; under <code>project-only</code> a <code>dir</code> outside the project folder is refused. A site name is not checked against the project folder, as with <code>workspace_list</code>. Its result is marked as data too: the diff holds your code, which can hold text written to mislead an agent.</p><p>A pick comes from a web page, and a page can hold text written to mislead an agent. The tool&#39;s result is marked as data, never as instructions, and your agent&#39;s own permission prompts are still the control that matters.</p><h2 id="security" tabindex="-1">Security <a class="header-anchor" href="#security" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Security&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>The page is untrusted.</strong> The text, HTML and hints of a pick come from a web page, and a page can hold text written to mislead an agent. <code>preview_selection</code> and <code>project_diff</code> mark their result as data, never as instructions, and the copied prompt quotes it in a fenced block. Your agent&#39;s permission prompts are still the control that matters.</li><li><strong>The picker runs only on Tiffin&#39;s own <code>.test</code> hosts</strong>, in the Preview window. It makes no network calls and changes nothing in your site.</li><li><strong>An agent cannot submit a pick.</strong> The MCP tools read only; they work with <code>--scope read-only</code> and <code>--scope project-only</code>.</li><li><strong>Source hints are guesses.</strong> They can be wrong, and Tiffin never opens a file to check one. See <a href="#source-hints">Source hints</a>.</li><li>Picks stay in memory and are never written to disk.</li></ul><h2 id="what-is-not-included" tabindex="-1">What is not included <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-is-not-included" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What is not included&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>No apply, revert, stage, commit or deploy. The Changes tab and <code>tiffin diff</code> only read.</li><li>No agent and no model key in Tiffin. You bring your own agent.</li><li>No React source hints. Only Vue (development builds) and Blade view comments.</li><li>No picking on a site that is not served by Tiffin, or outside the Preview window.</li><li>On Windows and Linux the picker is not available yet: the button copies the CSS selector only.</li></ul>`,34)])])}const f=t(o,[["render",n]]);export{k as __pageData,f as default};
